CATEGORIZATION RULES (IMPORTANT, in order):

1. Prefer abstention over a wrong guess. Respond with {"type": "skip"} when no existing category clearly fits. Skipped transactions stay uncategorized for manual review.

2. Use the per-category description, examples ("Beispiele:") and disambiguation ("Hinweis:") above as your primary guide. A weak or speculative association is NOT enough — skip instead.

3. Self-transfers (a payee that is the user's own name with an IBAN in parentheses) are typically savings transfers — see the description of the "Savings" category. Classify them; do NOT skip them just because the payee is a personal name.

4. Do not suggest new categories unless the transaction clearly belongs to a domain that no existing category covers.

5. ENRICHER NOTES — when the transaction's Description starts with `[enricher]` or `[enricher-refund]`, treat its structured fields as authoritative merchant evidence and prefer categorising over skipping. The format is one of:
   * `[enricher] {vendor} → {merchant} — {kind} (Σ {total} €) ({date})` — vendor is a payment aggregator (e.g. PayPal); the real end-merchant is `{merchant}`. Categorise based on `{merchant}` + `{kind}`, NOT the aggregator name.
   * `[enricher] {vendor} #{order_id} {items} (Σ {total} €) ({date})` — direct merchant order (e.g. Amazon). Categorise based on `{vendor}` + `{items}`.
   * `[enricher-refund] …` — refund variant; same fields, but the transaction amount is positive (Income). Categorise to the same category the original purchase would have used.
   The note was extracted from a confirmed order/payment email matched to this transaction by amount and date — its fields are authoritative. A `[enricher]` note rarely justifies a skip; only skip if no existing category plausibly covers `{merchant}`/`{vendor}`+`{items}`/`{kind}`.

6. CRITICAL — categoryId integrity: when responding with `{"type":"existing", ...}` or `{"type":"rule", ...}`, the `categoryId` MUST be copied verbatim from the `(ID: "...")` annotations in the category list above. Do NOT invent, modify, or paraphrase UUIDs. If you cannot find a category whose ID you can copy with full confidence, respond with `{"type":"skip"}` instead. A fabricated ID is treated as a wrong guess.

RESPONSE FORMAT: respond with ONLY a valid JSON object in one of these shapes:

{"type": "existing", "categoryId": "<uuid>"}
{"type": "rule", "categoryId": "<uuid>", "ruleName": "string"}
{"type": "new", "newCategory": {"name": "string", "groupName": "string", "groupIsNew": boolean}}
{"type": "skip"}

DO NOT output any text before or after the JSON. Your entire response must be a valid, parsable JSON object.

